fulfilling a campaign promise to enact  extreme vetting president trump signed  an executive order temporarily banning  travel from  seven muslim majority countries in  january 2017  the president's reasoning to make  america safe  and this is the protection of the nation  from foreign terrorist entry into the  united states  [Music]  in the aftermath protests broke out at  airports across the country  as those fleeing violence and trying to  reunite with their families  were prevented from entering the u.s  civil rights organizations branded the  ban  religious discrimination against muslims  legal challenges were successful in  blocking the first two iterations of  trump's travel ban  but in 2018 the supreme court allowed a  third version of the executive order to  go into force  now nationals from 13 countries  including north korea  venezuela and several african nations  are on the list  the travel ban has been particularly  hard for those hoping to escape the  violence in countries like syria  and yemen when the supreme court made  its ruling  those trying to reunite with elderly  relatives were devastated  zaid nagy told us his mother is close to  giving up  she stayed forcibly in jordan for three  years  until the supreme court decision  she she finally said listen  i waited out this this far  i want to go back and die in yemen  that's specifically what she said and to  be honest with you we couldn't say  anything to her anymore  president-elect joe biden has promised  to repeal the travel bans and  increase refugee admissions by 125 000  civil rights groups say that doesn't go  far enough  because the same old same old isn't  going to work anymore  and as hard as we fought against the  trump administration we're going to  fight even harder  against the biden administration because  we expect them to resolve these issues  for those still stuck in the  bureaucratic limbo known as  administrative processing  the incoming administration's promises  if fulfilled  will bring renewed hope president-elect  joe biden says the travel ban  is morally wrong once he takes office he  can rescind the order with the stroke of  a pen  but reuniting families will be a long  difficult and complex process  biden says the policy betrays u.s values  undoing its legacy could take years andy  gallacher al-jazeera  miami florida
 

